  "narrative": [
    {
      "text": "Knot theory's odd origins (Silver, American Scientist 94 (2006) 158–165) — how Kelvin's vortex atoms led Tait, Kirkman and Little to tabulate knots\n\n**Knot Theory's Odd Origins** — Daniel S. Silver. American Scientist 94(2) (2006) 158–165.\n\nPopular history: Tait's smoke-ring demonstrations (1867), Thomson's vortex-atom hypothesis, Maxwell's role, the theological context of *The Unseen Universe*, Tait's programme of knot tabulation and what knots were taken to mean. Ends with the eclipse of the vortex atom and the survival of knot tables as mathematics.\n\n**Why it may bear here.** Registered as `silver2006knotorigins`; cited in the knotted-particles history. A secondary source; `silliman1963-smoke-rings` and `kragh2002vortexatom` are the scholarly accounts. Fourteen images are not yet described.",
      "lang": "en",
